SOURCE NOTE: found while writing the #332 Reconstruct Itinerary benchmark, checking whether Kara had an in-place idiom for the adjacency build so the mirror comparison would not hand Kara an unfair handicap. REPRO (all at karac 227de29, release):

  let mut mi: Map[i64, Vec[i64]] = Map.new();
  let mut w: Vec[i64] = Vec.new(); w.push(1i64);
  let _ = mi.insert(7i64, w);
  mi[7i64].push(2i64);

`karac check` => All checks passed. `karac run --interp` => correct (len becomes 2). `karac run` (LLJIT) and `karac build` => `error: codegen failed: codegen: indexed-receiver method 'push' on 'mi' — outer is not a Vec/Slice/Array`. Identical failure with a String key. The control case `vv[0i64].push(2i64)` for `vv: Vec[Vec[i64]]` compiles and runs correctly under `karac build`, printing len=2 — so codegen's indexed-receiver path supports Vec/Slice/Array outers only.

investigation:
This is a run/build divergence in the repo's non-negotiable sense (interp succeeds, build fails), though it fails LOUDLY with an actionable hint rather than miscompiling — hence medium, not high. No wrong answer is produced on any surface.

WHY IT MATTERS BEYOND ERGONOMICS: without this form, the natural map-of-Vec accumulate must be written as get-copy-push-insert:

  let mut d = match adj.get(k) { Some(v) => v, None => Vec.new() };
  d.push(x);
  let _ = adj.insert(k, d);

Because `Map.get` yields a COPY rather than a reference, that idiom is O(degree) per edge insert instead of O(1) amortized, i.e. the adjacency build is O(sum deg^2) where Rust's `entry().or_default().push()`, Go's `append(m[k], x)` and Python's `setdefault` are all O(E). For #332's benchmark graph (1000 edges, JFK degree 40) the copy-back costs roughly 820 extra Vec clones per iteration on the hub vertex alone. So the gap is not only a missing convenience form — it forces every map-of-collection accumulate in the corpus onto a quadratic path, and it makes cross-language benchmark comparisons on that shape measure an API limitation rather than codegen quality.

NEXT STEP: extend codegen's indexed-receiver method lowering to accept a Map outer, resolving `m[k]` to the in-place value slot (the bucket payload address) rather than a loaded copy, then dispatch the method against it. The Vec/Slice/Array path already does exactly this against the element address; the Map case additionally needs the key lookup to produce a slot pointer and to fail/insert-default on a missing key (decide which — the interpreter currently requires the key to already be present).

MEASURED COST (kara-katas #332 benchmark, x86-64 container, 2026-07-25): on a 1000-edge Eulerian graph with a degree-40 hub, 250 iterations, kara runs 301.5ms +/- 12.7 against rust 164.1 +/- 10.2, go 128.2 +/- 7.7 and c 72.8 +/- 6.2 -- the first kata in the corpus where kara is last among the compiled languages. Re-running the SAME program on a graph with the same edge count but MAX DEGREE 1 (one long cycle, which removes the O(sum deg^2) term entirely) gives kara 229.8 +/- 6.4 vs rust 162.2 +/- 14.6. Rust is unchanged between the two shapes (inside sigma), so the graph shape costs it nothing; kara drops 71.7ms. That is ~52% of kara's 137ms deficit attributable to THIS bug, with a residual 1.42x that is genuine (String-keyed map ops, per-edge string copies, allocation). Expect ~52% of the gap on that kata to close when m[k].push(x) lowers, with no algorithmic change to the kata.

