MECHANISM (interp): `invoke_zero_arg_closure` maps the closure body's Err(ControlFlow::Break) through `set_cf`, which converts it to the PENDING-flag channel and yields Unit as the with_provider value; the pending Break then short-circuits the enclosing evaluations in a way the for-loop's catch never translates back into a loop exit with the accumulated state — the fn falls out with Unit.

DESIGN TENSION, decide before fixing: `return` inside the with_provider closure is CLOSURE-scoped per both the typechecker (the closure's return type is inferred from its `return` statements; `let x = with_provider(..., || { return read(); }); x + 1` typechecks and --interp gives 9) and the interpreter (invoke_zero_arg_closure catches ControlFlow::Return). Under closure-scoped semantics, a `break` targeting a loop OUTSIDE the closure should arguably be REJECTED at typecheck (there is no enclosing loop inside the closure — Rust's rule). Codegen instead compiles the body inline and gives break TRANSPARENT semantics (targets the enclosing loop; B-2026-07-31-11's cleanup-action pop makes it sound). Options: (a) reject break/continue crossing the closure boundary at typecheck — consistent with closure-scoped return, smallest surface; or (b) spec transparent-inline semantics and fix the interpreter to break the enclosing loop (then reconcile with closure-scoped return, which transparency contradicts — see B-2026-07-31-16). Either way the interpreter's silent Unit is wrong today. `e2e_with_provider_enclosing_loop_break_pops_frame` (tests/codegen.rs) pins codegen's current type-sound behaviour and carries a comment pointing here; update it if (a) is chosen.

