MINIMAL REPRO (probe c14_neg2, first swept 2026-08-01 — the c-series probes were never in the standing corpus sweep before, so this is a PRE-EXISTING divergence, not a regression; verified conceptually unreachable by the b178 fix, whose gate needs a for-loop element binding and this program has no for loop):
  fn main() {
      let outer: Vec[String] = Vec.new();
      let mut take = |x: String| {
          let mut v = outer;
          v.push(x);
          v.len()
      };
      let a = take(String.from("a"));
      let b = take(String.from("b"));
      println(a + b);
  }
  interp: 3    JIT: 188975220501568 (garbage, varies)    AOT: same garbage class — silent wrong output, no crash.

TWO DISTINCT DEFECTS IN ONE SHAPE:
  (1) SEMANTIC HOLE: `let mut v = outer` inside the closure body moves the captured binding ON EVERY CALL. The second `take(..)` call moves `outer` again after the first call consumed it. Neither the ownership checker nor the typechecker flags the closure as single-call (FnOnce-like) or the capture as consumed — `karac check` is silent. Rust would reject the closure as FnOnce called twice.
  (2) CODEGEN MISCOMPILE: whatever semantics one picks, both compiled backends agree with each other and disagree with the interpreter, printing a pointer-looking i64 — the closure env slot for `outer` is read as the Vec len (or v.len() reads a stale/aliased env word) after the first call zeroed/moved the env copy. The interpreter re-reads the env's captured value each call (still empty after the move: a=1, b=2, a+b=3).

RESOLUTION PATH (pick one, both legs must land together): EITHER reject at check time (a closure whose body moves a captured binding is single-call; a second call site is an error — the diagnostics-friendly option and the one design.md's ownership tiers imply), OR define capture-re-read semantics (each call re-reads the env copy) and make codegen implement it — in which case interp's 3 is the oracle. Either way the silent-garbage lane is the bug.

SOURCE NOTE: c-series recon probe c14_neg2 (b166-era closure/enum recon corpus), first flagged when the 2026-08-01 b178 sweep widened the corpus glob from b1*.kara to b1*+c*.kara (163 probes).

FIXED 8ab3b9a — with a ROOT-CAUSE CORRECTION: the garbage output was NOT the double-move (that is a separate, still-open defect, split to B-2026-08-01-27). The garbage was NAME SHADOWING: `take` is the spliced stdlib `std.mem::take[T: Default](dest: mut ref T) -> T`, and codegen's `compile_call` dispatched the bare-identifier call through the generic-fn table BEFORE the local closure-binding check — so `take(x)` compiled a direct call to the stdlib fn (reads *dest's first word, zeroes it, returns the old value: the param itself for i64, its data POINTER for String — the pointer-looking garbage), while the typechecker and interpreter both resolved the LOCAL closure (locals-first), a three-way divergence. Probes proved the double-call irrelevant: a SINGLE call garbles identically, a no-capture closure named `take` garbles identically, and RENAMING the closure removes the garbage entirely. The interpreter had the sibling hole for intercepted builtin names (`let spawn = |x| x + 1; spawn(4)` ran stdlib spawn and returned a TaskHandle). FIX (locals shadow, uniformly): codegen hoists a `closure_fn_types` + live-local-slot check above every name-keyed intercept and the generic-fn/fn-table dispatch in `compile_call` (gated on no explicit generic args; module-binding closures keep their later dispatch); the interpreter skips its whole builtin-intercept match when the name is bound to a local `Value::Function`; the typechecker's three unguarded intercepts (todo/unreachable, collect_all, println/print/eprintln) gain the same `local_scope.lookup` guard its spawn/ref_eq/distinct-ctor intercepts already had. Verified: closures named take (i64 + String), spawn, swap, replace all dispatch to the closure on all three backends; UNshadowed stdlib take still works ('5 0' on all three); cross-fn probe (closure take in fn a, stdlib take in fn b) correct on all three — the live-slot gate holds even though codegen's closure_fn_types is not per-function-cleared; valgrind 0 on all six probe b