SOURCE NOTE: found building the S3-client dogfood. The plan was a self-contained artifact — sign a request, serve it, verify the signature in-process — so it needs no AWS credentials or network. The server and client in one process is what breaks.

REPRO (11 lines, aborts on every run):
  struct Response { status: i64, body: String }
  fn handle(req: Request) -> Response { Response { status: 200, body: "x" } }
  fn serve_bg() { let _ = Server.serve("127.0.0.1:8094", handle); }
  fn main() with sends(Network) receives(Network) {
      let h = spawn(|| serve_bg());
      sleep_ms(400);
      let c = Client.new();
      match c.get("http://127.0.0.1:8094/p") {
          Ok(r) => { println("got"); }
          Err(e) => { println("err"); }
      }
  }

  -> BOUND_PORT=8094
     free(): double free detected in tcache 2   (SIGABRT; 3/3 runs)

WHICH ALLOCATION, PROVEN RATHER THAN GUESSED. The double-freed block's size tracks the HANDLER'S RESPONSE BODY exactly:
    body "x"                                  -> "block of size 1"
    body "abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789" -> "block of size 36"
36 characters, 36 bytes. It is the response body buffer, not an incidental allocation.

valgrind attribution:
  Invalid free() at the match site (generated code, main)
  Address … free'd earlier by karac_runtime_http_client_get
So the runtime's client path frees the body, and codegen's drop for the matched
`Result[Response, …]` frees it again. In a single process the server-produced buffer and the
client-consumed buffer ARE THE SAME ALLOCATION, and both sides claim ownership.

REDUCTION — what is and is not required (each measured):
  REQUIRED  a Kāra `Server.serve` running in the same process (in a `spawn`)
  REQUIRED  the client request to SUCCEED — a request to a dead port with the same server
            spawned is clean, so the failure path does not take it
  NOT req.  an external origin: the identical client code against a Python http.server is
            CLEAN. Cross-process is fine; same-process is not.
  NOT req.  reading the response — the Ok arm binds `r` and never touches it
  not alone spawn+server with no client is clean; client with no server is clean; client
            against an external server is clean. Only the in-process pair aborts.

WHY IT MATTERS: an in-process client+server is the natural shape for a self-contained example, an integration test, or any program that both serves and calls out — which is most real services. It is also the only way to write a self-checking network artifact that needs no external dependency, so it blocks that whole class of dogfood. And because the external-origin path is clean, the existing `tests/http_server.rs` client tests (which all use a Rust or external origin) structurally cannot catch it.

NOTE ON A NEARBY NON-BUG: `Server.serve` under `karac run --interp` reports a CLEAN structured diagnostic ("path 'Server.serve' has no interpreter evaluation rule … please report this"), so the tree-walk gap is handled, not a crash. This entry is codegen/JIT only.

── ROOT CAUSE FOUND, ORIGINAL FRAMING WAS WRONG (same day, before any fix) ──
This entry blamed the IN-PROCESS client/server pair. That is not the cause — it was a coincidence of the repro. The real trigger is a NAME COLLISION, and the in-process server was merely why my repro happened to declare the colliding type.

DECISIVE TEST — no spawn, no Kāra server, an ordinary external origin (python3 -m http.server), and the struct is NEVER REFERENCED:

  struct Response { status: i64, body: String }      <- declared, unused
  fn main() with sends(Network) receives(Network) {
      let c = Client.new();
      match c.get("http://127.0.0.1:8097/") {
          Ok(r) => { println("got"); }
          Err(e) => { println("err"); }
      }
  }
  -> free(): double free detected in tcache 2

  Rename the struct to `MyResp`, change nothing else -> clean, valgrind 0 errors.

BOUNDARY (each measured against an external origin):
  struct Response { status: i64, body: String }   DOUBLE FREE
  struct Response { status: i64 }                 clean   <- no heap field, drop glue
                                                            frees nothing
  struct Request  { path: String }                clean   <- `Response` specifically;
                                                            not general builtin shadowing
  no user struct at all                           clean

So: a user type named exactly `Response` that owns a heap field causes codegen to run THAT type's drop glue over the client's builtin response value, freeing a `String` buffer the client path already owns. valgrind confirms the shape — the block is alloc'd inside `karac_runtime_http_client_get` (whose ownership contract is correct and transfers to the caller) and then freed TWICE by generated code. The runtime is not at fault.

WHY THE IN-PROCESS FRAMING LOOKED RIGHT: a program that serves must declare a `Response` struct — that is the documented handler signature — so every in-process client+server program collides by construction, while my external-origin control had no such declaration. Two variables moved at once.

REACH IS WIDER THAN THIS ENTRY IMPLIED. Any program that BOTH serves and calls out hits it, because the serving half is required to declare `Response`. `examples/shortener/shortener.kara` already declares `struct Response { status, body, headers }` and is safe today only because it never uses `Client`. Adding one outbound call to it would corrupt memory, and nothing would warn.

FIX SHAPE (unverified): the client's response value must not be bound to a user layout by NAME. Either resolve the builtin response to its own internal type regardless of user declarations, or reject/shadow-warn a user struct named `Response` in a module that uses the HTTP client. The first is preferable — users should be free to name a struct `Response`, which is an extremely natural name in exactly this domain.

Found while auditing the sibling `into_struct_value()` coercions after closing B-2026-08-02-13. A `Server.serve` handler whose `Response` lacks a body field crashes codegen:

  struct Response { status: i64 }
  fn handle(req: Request) -> Response { Response { status: 200 } }
  fn main() with sends(Network) receives(Network) { Server.serve("127.0.0.1:0", handle); }

  -> thread panicked at src/codegen/http.rs:208:14

The ABI shim extracts field 0 as the status and field 1 as the body String; on a one-field struct the `build_extract_value(.., 1, ..).unwrap()` blows up with a raw Rust backtrace, violating the never-panic rule.

Distinct from B-2026-08-02-13 in an important way: that bug's guard refuses user structs that shadow a built-in on the CLIENT path, but `Server.serve` deliberately ACCEPTS a user-declared `Response` — that is the documented pattern and examples/shortener depends on it — so the shadowing guard cannot cover this path by design. The shape has to be validated instead of refused.

FIXED 7068f8c: `require_http_handler_response_shape` checks the handler's return type at all four shim call sites and reports what is required ("must return a `Response` shaped like the built-in one — a status field followed by a body `String` — but the returned struct has 1 field(s), so it has no body field"). Regression test in tests/http_client_codegen.rs asserts both that the diagnostic appears and that it is not a panic; examples/shortener still builds, confirming the legitimate user-declared-Response pattern is undisturbed.

