    fn s_of(i: i64) -> String { let mut s = String.new(); s.push_str("payload-"); s.push_str(f"{i}"); return s; }
    fn mkx(i: i64) -> Sx { let s = s_of(i); return Sx { msg: s }; }   // u1
    fn mkx(i: i64) -> Sx { return Sx { msg: s_of(i) }; }              // u2
  BOTH abort, both at 3 allocs / 4 frees. So the local is irrelevant. What distinguishes them from the one GENUINE clean control (b232/n2, 3 allocs / 3 frees) is that n2 initializes the field with an INLINE f-string expression -- constructed in place into the struct field, never existing as an owned temp -- while u1/u2/p1a/r8/n1 all obtain the field value from something that produces an OWNED TEMP OR BINDING (a call, or a local).  STATEMENT: a payload struct whose heap field is obtained from a call or a binding (rather than built in place) has that value's ownership DOUBLE-COUNTED once the struct becomes a fresh-temp `Result` payload that a match arm binds. Valgrind places both frees in `main`, eleven bytes apart, on the same block -- so they are the payload's own registrar and the bound binding's struct drop, not anything inside the constructor (which the IR already showed correctly zeroes its moved source's cap).  SURVIVING CLEAN CONTROLS, all with real allocations: n2 (inline-built field, 3/3), q4 (named scrutinee, 3/3), n7 (wildcard arm, 2/2), s1 and s2 (same construction, no `Result` match, 2/2). Discard r3, r4, p1b and the r6 half of the r6/r8 pair as vacuous.  NEXT STEP is now narrow: find who registers the call/binding-produced field value as owned on the way into a fresh-temp `Result` payload, and why the arm binding's `track_struct_var` does not displace it. Everything else in this row has been eliminated by measurement.  MECHANISM PINNED AT BLOCK LEVEL (2026-08-04, IR of b232/u2 vs n2 vs n7, `main` only). Mapping each free and each suppression to its BASIC BLOCK is what finally showed it -- the earlier IR reads compared instruction listings, which are in emission order, not execution order, and that is why they concluded "identical".  
    u2 (ABORTS)  suppress-group -> match.body0   struct-drop -> match.body1
    n2 (clean)   suppress-group -> match.body0   struct-drop -> cleanup.skip54
    n7 (clean)   suppress-group -> match.body0   struct-drop -> (none)
